  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Frost/freeze conditions are probable late tonight \nand early Tuesday morning over the western and central \nAlleghenies \n\nAs of 0730Z, a light southwesterly breeze developing just ahead\nof a SEWD-pushing cold front was helping to erode the northern\nand western edge of the widespread stratus deck covering much of\nCentral and Eastern PA. Patchy dense valley fog was occurring\nover and just SE of the Allegheny Front in Scent PA - in the \nnarrow transition zone between the initial breaks and slight\nincrease in SW wind. The relatively minor fog problems will\ndevelop a bit more to the east (toward the Lower Susq Valley)\nthrough 11Z before improvement begins. \n\nTiming of the cold front goes like this - the boundary will push\nthrough the Northwest Mountains during the early morning hours \ntoday, crossing the Laurel Highlands and Central Mountains \nshortly after sunrise and the Lower Susquehanna Valley late this\nmorning.\n\nA rather deep upper level trough over northern New York state \nwill deepen overnight, with a tight 500 mb height gradient by \nmorning. This will lead to bands of lower SC clouds and gusty \nwinds early Monday, but 500 mb heights come up fast later in the\nday. The rising 500 mb heights will aid in a cold night Monday \nNight, as winds slacken, and clouds diminish with the warming \naloft and much drier air being advected into central PA.\n\nThe coldest air of the early Autumn season will spread southeast\nacross Central PA early this week with frost/freeze conditions \nexpected tonight into early Tuesday morning over much of the \nwestern and central Alleghenies (northwest of Interstate-81). \n\nWe maintained the Freeze Watch for 4 NW PA counties for late\ntonight/early Tuesday (as there is some questions whether there\nwill be widespread near to slightly below freezing temps or lows\n33-35F INVOF KBFD and other perennial cold spots across the NW\nMtns). \n\nA Frost Advisory was issued for the Laurel Highlands, and \nportions of the Central and North-Central Mtns. Continued to \nlean a toward slightly colder guidance in the form of a NBM/MOS\nblend. Placement of sfc high becomes somewhat less favorable \nfor frost/freeze Tuesday night.\n\n\n-------------------------------------------------------------\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2: Extended stretch of dry and seasonable Fall \nweather this week\n\nLow clouds and patchy dense valley fog will cover much the \nCentral Ridge and Valley Region of the state and Susquehanna \nValley early today.\n\nConditions will improve sharply around and shortly after \nsunrise as the mean low level flow shifts from the \neast/southeast to the west/northwest behind a secondary cold \nfront. The colder WNW flow could bring a couple of rain showers \ndownwind of Lake Erie tonight into early Monday.\n\nOtherwise, there is high confidence in a prolonged stretch of \ndry and seasonable Fall wx this week (Mon- Fri) as a large area\nof Canadian high pressure dominates the pattern.\n\nThe next chance of showers will likely arrive Sunday.",
